Using the Script Plugin to the Delivery XML Editor
--------------------------------------------------
This README explains how to use the Script Plugin to the Delivery XML 
Editor.  General instructions on Plugin construction and use can be found 
in Plugin_README.

The Script Plugin (run by adding the command line argument 
plugin=script/ScriptPlugin) creates one new menu to the menu bar at the top 
and modifies how things are edited, and if you include a -r command line 
option, restricts what can be edited.  

Command Line Options
----------------------
    -r      			Launch Script Editor in restricted mode
    plugin-hs="helpset_name"	Load a helpset from file or classpath location
				"helpset_name"

First I'll explain editing.  The nodes called parameters and parameter have 
special edit modes where a dialog opens up with a table listing either 
the one parameter to edit if you chose to edit a parameter, or all parameters 
if you chose to edit the parameters node.  Simply click in the boxes you wish 
to edit, and then choose "Accept" when you're done.  A word of warning, 
though.  If java thinks you are currently editing a cell (true so long as that 
vertical bar exists), it won't register it as changed and so when you choose 
"Accept" the parameter will remain the same.  Just click on another box before 
you click "Accept" to be safe.  I will try to fix this feature later if 
I have time.  If -r was entered as a command line option, only parameters and 
parameter nodes will be editable, and nothing will be cuttable, copyable, 
insertable, or pasteable.  In this mode you can only change the values of the 
parameters.  

For those editing without -r, you will build or modify a chain of commands 
interspersed with scatter and gather nodes.  The nodes have icons designed to 
help you monitor the flow of the job, but until you try to create a script no 
real error checking is done that way.  But it should be clear following the 
arrows if some appear to be unhooked.  A scatter and a gather node follow the 
same restrictions as stand-alone commands:  they must occur after the end of 
a pipe or another stand-alone command, and they must be followed by a 
stand-alone command or the start of a pipe.

Once you've finished editing, it's time to generate or run the script.  Under 
the bsub scripts menu, you have choices "Show script", "Generate script", and 
"Execute script".  This do precisely what the sound like.  "Show script" 
displays the top-level script that would be generated by "Generate script" but 
with a generic name.  (Note: currently it produces scripts of the same 
generic name in the current directory...so probably you should just do 
generate.)  "Generate Script" pops up a dialog asking you what directory to 
place the scripts in and to provide a base name for the scripts.  The major 
script to run will be baseName.sh or baseName_bsub.sh for the multi-processor 
bsub version.  "Exectute Script" first behaves like "Generate Script", then 
runs the generated script in the current directory.  (Note: later the user 
will specify the run directory).  "Execute Script" also gives you options on 
how to run the script, like forcing a linear, one-processor run.

